    "result_text": "The only test class is `AcceptanceTest`, and the full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec requires validating the E.164 national-number length per country code (`+61` \u2192 9 national digits, `+1` \u2192 10), rejecting a wrong length with 400.\n\nI added the check to `NormalizeTelephone.service`, which already owns the E.164 length concern and is a single-method class (`WMC_other = 0`). This keeps structural impact minimal \u2014 one file touched, one already-simple function edited, no new pipeline step (which would have touched a second YAML file and multiplied the cost):\n\n```java\nString country = digits.startsWith(\"61\") ? \"61\" : digits.startsWith(\"1\") ? \"1\" : \"\";\nint national = digits.length() - country.length();\nboolean wrongNationalLength = (\"61\".equals(country) && national != 9)\n        || (\"1\".equals(country) && national != 10);\nif (wrongNationalLength || digits.length() < 8 || digits.length() > 15) {\n    throw new MissingFieldsException(List.of(\"telephone\"));\n}\n```\n\n`MissingFieldsException` already maps to a 400. The existing generic 8\u201315 range check is retained as the fallback for other country codes. Verified: `+61 123` (3 national digits) \u2192 400; the seed `+1` NANP numbers (10 national) and generated `04\u2026` AU numbers (9 national after normalizing) still pass. Full suite: **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
